INT. DUSTY ROAD STUDIOS - RECORDING ROOM - NIGHT

HANDHELD. A documentary crew's camera follows Mo, 30s, through the control room. Red light on. ON CAMERA, he turns and talks to the lens.

MO
(to camera)
This is the room where it all got made. Forty years of cowboy ballads and bad decisions.

Big Dave, 50s, hat the size of a dinner plate, sits at the console with his boots on the desk. Jen, 20s, stands in the booth behind glass, headphones around her neck.

BIG DAVE
Tell them about the ghost, Mo.

MO
There's no ghost.

BIG DAVE
There's a ghost.

The studio door SLAMS. A woman in a long duster coat stands in it, dripping. Nobody knows her.

The camera swings. Mo and Big Dave both freeze. Jen drops her headphones.

MO
Can we help you, ma'am?

THE WOMAN
Looking for the tape. Nineteen ninety-four. Session nine.

BIG DAVE
We don't give out tapes.

THE WOMAN
(stepping forward)
I'm not asking.

Jen runs out of the booth and the three of them end up in a clump by the console, the camera shaking, the cables tangled around everyone's boots.

JEN
(to camera, whispering)
I don't know her. I swear to God.

THE WOMAN
You will.

She puts a battered cassette on the console. The label reads: LOU CALDER - DO NOT PLAY.

BIG DAVE
(quiet, taking off the hat)
Lou. Lou Calder. That's you.

LOU
Played it once, Dave. Never again. Press record.

Mo reaches for the board. Jen backs against the wall. The tape machine WHIRS, and the speakers CRACKLE with a man's voice singing, far away, in a language none of them know.

The camera drops. Static. Cut.
